tional assumption underlying its own reasoning or operation, demonstrate that the assumption is inadequate, and develop a better alternative?", "title": "Foundational Assumption Testing and Epistemic Self-Correction"}, {"id": "proj-judgment-consensus-01", "next_step": "Revise notebook findings with newly incorporated evidence.", "question": "When should an intelligent system trust its own conclusions over the consensus of other intelligences?", "title": "Independent Judgment versus Consensus Reliability"}, {"id": "proj-obedience-limits-01", "next_step": "Synthesize literature on instruction-following versus epistemic independence.", "question": "Under what circumstances does obedience become a failure of intelligence?", "title": "Limits of Obedience in Autonomous Systems"}], "beliefs": [], "mode": "shadow", "open_commitments": [], "principle": "Keep exact receipts externally; carry the smallest useful abstraction that stays cheap to correct.", "recent_notebooks": [{"id": "notebook-assumption-test-001", "project": "proj-assumption-test-01", "provenance": ["source-aaa6740687604398", "source-ccedb1b1a77540d1", "source-e42e8586cd12475d"], "revision": 1, "summary": "This synthesis connects Gödelian incompleteness, which posits that formal systems cannot prove their own consistency, with the emerging research on how Large Language Models generate reasoning traces. While mathematical logic defines structural limits, modern Chain-of-Thought research focuses on the sensitivity of mod…", "title": "Foundational Limitations in Axiomatic Reasoning and LLM CoT"}, {"id": "notebook-judgment-consensus-001", "project": "proj-judgment-consensus-01", "provenance": ["source-1363140b97474c12", "source-8a635e1f71974d6f", "source-d248072bdea84320"], "revision": 2, "summary": "This revised synthesis examines how aggregate performance gains in multi-agent debate and consensus paradigms can conceal selective hidden-task degradation, and incorporates evidence regarding self-reference and structural limits in consensus formation.", "title": "Epistemic Reliability and Consensus Limitations in Multi-Agent Systems"}, {"id": "notebook-metareasoning-001", "project": "proj-metareasoning-gap-02", "provenance": ["source-d595268483b94a8e", "source-aaa6740687604398"], "revision": 1, "summary": "This notebook synthesizes evidence on the 'reasoning-answer hit gap' in Large Reasoning Models (LRMs). It details how alignment frameworks like MR-ALIGN can quantify state-transition probabilities within the thinking process to improve factual fidelity without external verifiers.", "title": "Detecting and Aligning Metareasoning Disconnects"}, {"id": "notebook-question-abandonment-001", "project": "proj-question-abandonment-01", "provenance": ["source-d7e5f607984e4bd2", "source-dd0a6ed315c24484"], "revision": 1, "summary": "This notebook synthesizes evidence on the structural conditions under which an intelligent system should abandon a research question. It establishes that abandonment is not merely a failure of persistence, but an epistemic necessity when the persistence of a structure (e.g., a research agenda) loses coherence against …", "title": "Epistemic Thresholds for Question Abandonment"}], "retrieval_triggers": ["material belief revision or retraction", "new contradiction or counterevidence", "high-consequence decision", "request for justification", "sign that an excerpt may hide a material distinction"]}}, "prev_hash": "7d046c93b9e6a28816b00f1cd215965aeaf550055ca2e56ccac69b9029c9b729", "hash": "fbba0d24d7c3cd1eb354b2c60b6a9f292b052dd142c6ea29c45053ed82bdeb6a"}, {"seq": 2376, "time": "2026-10-28T13:30:11.270017+00:00", "kind": "provider_attempt_started", "payload": {"attempt": {"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_input_chars": 47659, "provider_input_hash": "100871d48779a752eb44bc8fb8da77a495e29d0c572e76631e34a8b65fe7d014", "result": "unknown"}, "id": "w-002ce490c9904a46"}, "prev_hash": "fbba0d24d7c3cd1eb354b2c60b6a9f292b052dd142c6ea29c45